----- Test 3 of 7 -+- dir: . -+- tabs2spaces --help -----
usage: tabs2spaces [-h | --help] [OPTION]... [FILE]...

tabs2spaces is a wrapper for the command expand. The FILES are changed in place.

A backup copy of each original FILE is created called ".FILE.tabs" in the
directory that FILE resides.

Two special files are also created in the current directory to allow you
to review all the changes made as well as undo the changes if the results
weren't what you expected.  Namely, the files ".tabs.diff.tabs" and
".tabs.undo.tabs" which are executable.

Note: To get rid of all the crufty leftovers:
    find . -name '.*.tabs' -type f -print0 | xargs -0 /bin/rm -f

positional arguments:
  FILE                      The file(s) to modify

optional arguments:
  -h, --help                     Display this help and exit
  -t NUMBER, --tabs=NUMBER       expands/unexpands tabs by NUMBER characters - default 8.
  --strip-trailing-whitespace    also strip trailing whitespace from every line
  --no-strip-trailing-whitespace leave trailing whitespace untouched [default]
  --version                      print out the version number and exit

Tip: if your editor's tab width differs from the system default (most
systems assume 8), first run "tabs2spaces -t N" (using YOUR editor's
width) to convert indentation to spaces, then "spaces2tabs -t 8" to
re-tab at the standard width. This preserves how the file looks, even
for someone viewing it with an unmodified, default-width tool.

exit code: 0

----- Test 4 of 7 -+- dir: . -+- spaces2tabs --help -----
usage: spaces2tabs [-h | --help] [OPTION]... [FILE]...

spaces2tabs is a wrapper for the command unexpand. The FILES are changed in place.

A backup copy of each original FILE is created called ".FILE.tabs" in the
directory that FILE resides.

Two special files are also created in the current directory to allow you
to review all the changes made as well as undo the changes if the results
weren't what you expected.  Namely, the files ".tabs.diff.tabs" and
".tabs.undo.tabs" which are executable.

Note: To get rid of all the crufty leftovers:
    find . -name '.*.tabs' -type f -print0 | xargs -0 /bin/rm -f

positional arguments:
  FILE                      The file(s) to modify

optional arguments:
  -h, --help                     Display this help and exit
  -t NUMBER, --tabs=NUMBER       expands/unexpands tabs by NUMBER characters - default 8.
  --strip-trailing-whitespace    also strip trailing whitespace from every line
  --no-strip-trailing-whitespace leave trailing whitespace untouched [default]
  --version                      print out the version number and exit

Tip: if your editor's tab width differs from the system default (most
systems assume 8), first run "tabs2spaces -t N" (using YOUR editor's
width) to convert indentation to spaces, then "spaces2tabs -t 8" to
re-tab at the standard width. This preserves how the file looks, even
